"PRPO: Aligning Process Reward with Outcome Reward in Policy Optimization (https://arxiv.org/html/2601.07182v1)\nciteturn26951view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn26941view5\",\"lineno\":322}); Total lines: 494\nL209: We train on 8 H200 GPUs with batch size 128, learning rate $1\\times 10^{-6}$, KL loss coefficient 0.001, clip ratio 0.2, maximum new tokens 2048, and rollout number 8. For segmentation, we pick five entropy spikes that are at least ten tokens apart ($k{=}5,n{=}10$).\nL210: For PRM, we choose Qwen2.5-Math-PRM-7B as the PRM model, and deploy it on 8 H200 GPUs according to official guidance to normalize the output value with $[0,1]$ range. We did an analysis on the time cost of PRM when training Qwen-Math-1.5B in Table cite53†1 . In this experiment, we take GRPO as model training without PRM, and PRPO+GRPO as model training with PRM. We trained each model for 1 epoch and calculated the average time cost for each step.\nL211: Even with PRM and training three models simultaneously, the additional time cost is about 37 seconds each step (46.8 s vs 84.0 s). Comparing to the benefit and higher upper bound brought by PRM, the time cost increase is acceptable.\nL212: We compare PRPO with GRPO and PRM-Avg (cite36†Zou et al., 2025 ; cite37†Ren, 2025 ). PRM-Avg adds the average process reward to the outcome reward, which is a common practice in recent work of using PRM in RL training like ReasonFlux (cite36†Zou et al., 2025 ) and lsrl (cite37†Ren, 2025 ). It’s worth noting that there’s a slight difference between lsrl and reasonflux, where lsrl use a discounted sum of process reward, while reasonflux use naive average of process reward.\nL213: As reasonflux is a more recent and widely referenced work, we use naive average used in reasonflux for PRM-avg. As PRPO provides another insight of advantage calculation, it can apply to both baselines. We also compare our algorithm with the current state-of-the-art baseline PURE (cite38†Cheng et al., 2025 ) algorithm, which is the only critic-free algorithm that tried to realize process-level credit assignment in critic-free framework.\nL214: We implement PURE with the exact same reward and advantage settings in the original work to reach the optimal performance, where the output reward signal is 0-1 reward, and the assignment temperature is set as 0.1. We evaluate performance of each algorithm on Qwen2.5-Math-1.5B and Qwen2.5-Math-7B (cite52†Yang et al., 2024 ).\nL215: We conduct our algorithm and train the models with the VeRL training framework (cite54†Sheng et al., 2025 ).\nL216: Models are trained on the training split of MATH (cite55†Hendrycks et al., 2021 ) with 12000 samples with early stopping.\nL217: Evaluation covers MATH, AMC 2023, AIME 2025, and AIME 2024 (cite56†Art of Problem Solving, 2025a ; cite57†Art of Problem Solving, 2025b ) using greedy decoding (pass@1) for MATH as there is sufficient number of test cases in its test split (500 samples) and sampling 32 rollouts for AMC/AIME with temperature 0.7, top-p 0.9, and seeds fixed to 42 as there are limited test cases in AIME (30 samples each dataset) and AMC 2023 (40 samples).\nL218: For algorithms other than PURE, rule based outcome rewards are $\\pm 1$ based on SymPy-verified correctness, plus a length penalty beyond 1024 tokens. Process rewards come from Qwen2.5-Math-PRM-7B.\nL219: We draw the accuracy curve of different algorithms trained on Qwen2.5-Math-1.5B in Figure cite58†3 . As shown in the figure, PRPO steadily improves both baselines, and generally fit quicker and reach higher accuracy than all baselines.\nL220: \nL221: The length penalty is described as follows:\nL222: \nL223:  | $$\\text{length\\_penalty}=\\begin{cases}0&\\text{if }\\text{length}\\leq 1024\\\\\nL224: \\frac{\\text{length}}{1024}&\\text{if }\\text{length}>1024\\end{cases}$$  |\nL225: As shown in Table cite59†2 , our PRPO algorithm consistently outperforms both GRPO and PRM-Avg baselines across all evaluated datasets and model sizes. Qwen-M-1.5 refers to Qwen2.5-Math-1.5B model, and Qwen-M-7 refers to Qwen2.5-Math-7B model. For Qwen2.5-Math-1.5B, PRPO improves MATH pass@1 accuracy from 61.20% (GRPO) to 64.4% (+3.2%), and further to 66.00% when combined with PRM-Avg (64.00% vs 66.00%).\nL226: On AMC2023 and AIME2024, our method also yields substantial gains, demonstrating its effectiveness in both standard and challenging mathematical benchmarks. Although our method does not perform as well as PRM-Avg on AIME2025, the gap is not significant (5.9% vs 7.00%), which can be attributed to capacity and fitting limits under dense supervision.\nL227: PRM-Avg injects a dense per-segment signal; smaller or weaker models may not have enough capacity to disentangle noisy PRM scores from outcome supervision, effectively turning dense rewards into a length or variance penalty. This is especially pronounced on AIME2024 with the 7B model and on AIME2025 with the 1.5B model, where the tasks are harder and label noise is higher.\nL228: In contrast, larger models (7B on AIME2025) can better absorb and align dense signals, which explains why the degradation diminishes as capacity increases. Comparing to PURE (cite38†Cheng et al., 2025 ), our algorithm also demonstrates its robustness across all datasets.\nL229: We also did an experiment on different pass rate accuracy of Qwen2.5-Math-7B model trained with different methods. As shown in Table cite60†3 , our algorithm consistently outperforms both GRPO and PRM-Avg baselines across all evaluated datasets, which shows the stability and effectiveness of our algorithm when applied to models with enough parameters.\nL230: It is worth noting that PRMAVG usually underperforms GRPO on AIME2025 and AIME2024, which shows the lack of robustness of using average process reward as outcome reward. However, with PRPO, both PRMAVG and GRPO can generally obtain great improvement on all datasets under different pass rate, showing the robustness of our algorithm.\nL231: These results validate our hypothesis that aligning process-level and outcome-level rewards enables more stable and effective policy optimization, especially in multi-process reasoning tasks where sparse signals are insufficient for robust learning.\nL232: \nL233: Figure 4: Ablation on relative process-reward normalization.\nL234: Additionally, we did an ablation study on our entropy based split strategy. For comparison, we select random k points that are at least n tokens away from other points as random split, and equally divide the output sequence into k+1 parts as uniform split.\nL235: As shown in Table cite61†4 , our algorithm with random split can only reach 2.4% and our algorithm with uniform split can only reach 29.8% of accuracy in MATH dataset, which are significantly lower than naive GRPO (61.20%) and our algorithm with entropy based split (64.40%), which shows the essence of entropy based split in our algorithm.\nL236: We propose a possible reason to explain the the failure of random and uniform split strategies stems from the fact that these splits are not semantically-based: Within a single segment, there may exist both completely correct processes and completely incorrect processes. When such a segment receives a positive reward, the incorrect processes are incorrectly reinforced; conversely, when it receives a negative reward, the correct processes are incorrectly suppressed.\nL237: Regardless of how the reward is assigned, a portion of the credit assignment will inevitably be problematic, ultimately leading to the collapse of model training.\nL238: We also did an ablation study on our predefined distribution for process-level advantage calculation. For comparison, we use the relative distribution of process-level advantage, which means the mean and standard deviation used to normalize and calculate process-level advantage is calculated each time based on the process rewards in the rollout.\nL239: As shown in Table cite61†4 , our algorithm with relative distribution for process-level advantage can reach 64.0% of accuracy in MATH dataset and our algorithm with predefined distribution of process-level advantage can reach 64.4% of accuracy in MATH dataset, where there is no significant difference.\nL240: Nevertheless, we draw a graph of the change of accuracy in each epoch of our algorithm with relative distribution for process-level advantage and our algorithm with predefined distribution for process-level advantage in Figure cite62†3(a) and Figure cite63†4 , it can be seen that our algorithm with relative distribution for process-level advantage has its accuracy increase at first, but suddenly drop to nearly 0% since the 6th epoch, which shows the great instability of relative distribution for process-level advantage calculation.\nL241: We believe this is caused by the Premature Termination Problem we discussed in the previous section, when the model starts to reach a level of accuracy, some essential processes, which are still good but are relatively lower, can be an outlier and come with an extremely low advantage, finally leading to the collapse of the model.\nL242: This ablation experiment proves the essence of predefined distribution for process-level advantage calculation in our algorithm, in order to both improve performance and stability of RL training.\nL243: ## 5 Conclusion\nL244: \nL245: We present process Relative Policy Optimization, a critic-free method that aligns dense process rewards with sparse outcome rewards. PRPO normalizes PRM scores, aligns them with outcome advantages, and delivers stable per-token guidance. Experiments on mathematical reasoning benchmarks show consistent gains over GRPO and PRM-Avg while retaining critic-free efficiency.\nL246: ## 6 Limitations\nL247: PRPO mitigates sparse rewards in GRPO by integrating dense PRM feedback, but it depends on PRM quality. In our work, we proposed a hypothesis about the possible collapse when training with process-only reward, and provide a possible mathematical proof of this hypothesis (shown in Appendix cite20†A ) which is incomplete because a lack of definition of the output distribution of PRMs. A more rigorous proof can be done to prove the point and provide more insights about how to avoid the collapse.\nL248: The fixed prior mean and variance used for process-reward normalization assume PRM outputs lie in $[0,1]$, which is viable for most PRMs that normalize each output to $[0,1]$, but may not be applicable to PRMs with other ranges or distributions; extending normalization to PRMs with other ranges or distributions is an open direction. Future work will jointly optimize PRMs with the policy and explore adaptive segmentation that combines semantic cues.\nL249: ## 7 Potential Risks\nL250: There are potential risks in using PRPO, which is the same as the risks in using GRPO. However, PRPO is more robust than GRPO because it has a more stable distribution of process-level advantage, which is more likely to converge to a stable distribution.\nL251: For example, when the model starts to reach a level of accuracy, some essential processes, which are still good but are relatively lower, can be an outlier and come with an extremely low advantage that cannot be resolved by our distribution alignment mechanism, finally leading to the collapse of the model. This is a potential risk of using PRPO, and we believe it is a rare case and can be mitigated by using a more robust PRM or a more robust segmentation strategy.\nL252: ## References\nL253:   * Art of Problem Solving (2025a) Art of Problem Solving. 2025a. American invitational mathematics examination (aime). cite64†https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=AIME_Problems_and_Solutions†artofproblemsolving.com . Accessed: 2025-11-21.\nL254:   * Art of Problem Solving (2025b) Art of Problem Solving. 2025b. American invitational mathematics examination (aime). cite65†https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php/2023_AMC_12A_Problems†artofproblemsolving.com . Accessed: 2025-11-21.\nL255:   * Cao et al. (2025) Lang Cao, Renhong Chen, Yingtian Zou, Chao Peng, Huacong Xu, Yuxian Wang, Wu Ning, Qian Chen, Mofan Peng, Zijie Chen, Peishuo Su, Sirui Han, and Yitong Li. 2025. cite66†More bang for the buck: Process reward modeling with entropy-driven uncertainty . Preprint, arXiv:2503.22233.\nL256:   * Chen et al. (2025) Zengjue Chen, Runliang Niu, He Kong, Qi Wang, Qianli Xing, and Zipei Fan. 2025. cite67†Tgrpo :fine-tuning vision-language-action model via trajectory-wise group relative policy optimization . Preprint, arXiv:2506.08440.\nL257:   * Cheng et al. (2025) Jie Cheng, Gang Xiong, Ruixi Qiao, Lijun Li, Chao Guo, Junle Wang, Yisheng Lv, and Fei-Yue Wang. 2025. cite68†Stop summation: Min-form credit assignment is all process reward model needs for reasoning . Preprint, arXiv:2504.15275.\nL258:   * Dai et al. (2025) Ning Dai, Zheng Wu, Renjie Zheng, Ziyun Wei, Wenlei Shi, Xing Jin, Guanlin Liu, Chen Dun, Liang Huang, and Lin Yan. 2025. cite69†Process supervision-guided policy optimization for code generation . Preprint, arXiv:2410.17621.\nL259:   * Hendrycks et al. (2021) Dan Hendrycks, Collin Burns, Saurav Kadavath, Akul Arora, Steven Basart, Eric Tang, Dawn Song, and Jacob Steinhardt. 2021. cite70†Measuring mathematical problem solving with the math dataset . Preprint, arXiv:2103.03874.\nL260:   * Lai et al. (2024) Xin Lai, Zhuotao Tian, Yukang Chen, Senqiao Yang, Xiangru Peng, and Jiaya Jia. 2024. cite71†Step-dpo: Step-wise preference optimization for long-chain reasoning of llms . Preprint, arXiv:2406.18629.\nL261:   * Lin et al. (2025) Xingyu Lin, Yilin Wen, En Wang, Du Su, Wenbin Liu, Chenfu Bao, and Zhonghou Lv. 2025. cite72†Token-level policy optimization: Linking group-level rewards to token-level aggregation via markov likelihood . Preprint, arXiv:2510.09369.\nL262:   * Liu et al. (2025a) Jia Liu, ChangYi He, YingQiao Lin, MingMin Yang, FeiYang Shen, and ShaoGuo Liu. 2025a. cite73†Ettrl: Balancing exploration and exploitation in llm test-time reinforcement learning via entropy mechanism . Preprint, arXiv:2508.11356.\nL263:   * Liu et al. (2025b) Zichen Liu, Changyu Chen, Wenjun Li, Penghui Qi, Tianyu Pang, Chao Du, Wee Sun Lee, and Min Lin. 2025b. cite74†Understanding r1-zero-like training: A critical perspective . Preprint, arXiv:2503.20783.\nL264:   * Luo et al. (2024) Liangchen Luo, Yinxiao Liu, Rosanne Liu, Samrat Phatale, Meiqi Guo, Harsh Lara, Yunxuan Li, Lei Shu, Yun Zhu, Lei Meng, Jiao Sun, and Abhinav Rastogi. 2024. cite75†Improve mathematical reasoning in language models by automated process supervision . Preprint, arXiv:2406.06592.\nL265:   * Ren (2025) Hangliang Ren. 2025. Lsrl: Process-supervised grpo on latent recurrent states improves mathematical reasoning. In Findings of the Association for Computational Linguistics: EMNLP 2025, pages 12534–12545.\nL266:   * Ren et al. (2025) Tao Ren, Jinyang Jiang, Hui Yang, Wan Tian, Minhao Zou, Guanghao Li, Zishi Zhang, Qinghao Wang, Shentao Qin, Yanjun Zhao, Rui Tao, Hui Shao, and Yijie Peng. 2025. cite76†Riskpo: Risk-based policy optimization via verifiable reward for llm post-training . Preprint, arXiv:2510.00911.\nL267:   * Shao et al. (2024) Zhihong Shao, Peiyi Wang, Qihao Zhu, Runxin Xu, Junxiao Song, Xiao Bi, Haowei Zhang, Mingchuan Zhang, Y. K. Li, Y. Wu, and Daya Guo. 2024. cite77†Deepseekmath: Pushing the limits of mathematical reasoning in open language models . Preprint, arXiv:2402.03300.\nL268:   * Sheng et al. (2025) Guangming Sheng, Chi Zhang, Zilingfeng Ye, Xibin Wu, Wang Zhang, Ru Zhang, Yanghua Peng, Haibin Lin, and Chuan Wu. 2025. cite78†Hybridflow: A flexible and efficient rlhf framework†doi.org . In Proceedings of the Twentieth European Conference on Computer Systems, page 1279–1297. ACM.\nL269:   * Song et al. (2025) Mingyang Song, Zhaochen Su, Xiaoye Qu, Jiawei Zhou, and Yu Cheng. 2025. cite79†Prmbench: A fine-grained and challenging benchmark for process-level reward models . Preprint, arXiv:2501.03124.\nL270:   * Wang et al. (2024) Peiyi Wang, Lei Li, Zhihong Shao, Runxin Xu, Damai Dai, Yifei Li, Deli Chen, Yu Wu, and Zhifang Sui. 2024. Math-shepherd: Verify and reinforce llms step-by-step without human annotations. In Proceedings of the 62nd Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics (Volume 1: Long Papers), pages 9426–9439.\nL271:   * Wang et al. (2025) Shenzhi Wang, Le Yu, Chang Gao, Chujie Zheng, Shixuan Liu, Rui Lu, Kai Dang, Xionghui Chen, Jianxin Yang, Zhenru Zhang, Yuqiong Liu, An Yang, Andrew Zhao, Yang Yue, Shiji Song, Bowen Yu, Gao Huang, and Junyang Lin. 2025. cite80†Beyond the 80/20 rule: High-entropy minority tokens drive effective reinforcement learning for llm reasoning . Preprint, arXiv:2506.01939.\nL272:   * Wen et al. (2024) Muning Wen, Cheng Deng, Jun Wang, Weinan Zhang, and Ying Wen. 2024. cite81†Entropy-regularized token-level policy optimization for large language models†doi.org . CoRR, abs/2402.06700.\nL273:   * Xu et al. (2025) Huimin Xu, Xin Mao, Feng-Lin Li, Xiao Bao Wu, Wang Chen, Wei Zhang, and Anh Tuan Luu. 2025. cite82†Full-step-dpo: Self-supervised preference optimization with step-wise rewards for mathematical reasoning . In Findings of the Association for Computational Linguistics: ACL 2025.\nL274:   * Yang et al. (2024) An Yang, Beichen Zhang, Binyuan Hui, Bofei Gao, Bowen Yu, Chengpeng Li, Dayiheng Liu, Jianhong Tu, Jingren Zhou, Junyang Lin, Keming Lu, Mingfeng Xue, Runji Lin, Tianyu Liu, Xingzhang Ren, and Zhenru Zhang. 2024. cite83†Qwen2.5-math technical report: Toward mathematical expert model via self-improvement . Preprint, arXiv:2409.12122.\nL275:   * Zhang et al. (2025) Zhenru Zhang, Chujie Zheng, Yangzhen Wu, Beichen Zhang, Runji Lin, Bowen Yu, Dayiheng Liu, Jingren Zhou, and Junyang Lin. 2025. cite84†The lessons of developing process reward models in mathematical reasoning . Preprint, arXiv:2501.07301.\nL276:   * Zhu et al. (2025) Mingkang Zhu, Xi Chen, Zhongdao Wang, Bei Yu, Hengshuang Zhao, and Jiaya Jia. 2025. cite85†Tgdpo: Harnessing token-level reward guidance for enhancing direct preference optimization . Preprint, arXiv:2506.14574.\nL277:   * Zou et al. (2025) Jiaru Zou, Ling Yang, Jingwen Gu, Jiahao Qiu, Ke Shen, Jingrui He, and Mengdi Wang. 2025. cite86†Reasonflux-prm: Trajectory-aware prms for long chain-of-thought reasoning in llms . Preprint, arXiv:2506.18896.\nL278:   * Zuo et al. (2025) Yuxin Zuo, Kaiyan Zhang, Li Sheng, Shang Qu, Ganqu Cui, Xuekai Zhu, Haozhan Li, Yuchen Zhang, Xinwei Long, Ermo Hua, Biqing Qi, Youbang Sun, Zhiyuan Ma, Lifan Yuan, Ning Ding, and Bowen Zhou. 2025. cite87†Ttrl: Test-time reinforcement learning . Preprint, arXiv:2504.16084.\nL279: ## Appendix A Possible Mathematical Reason for Premature Collapse in Process-Only Reward cite88†1 L280: \nL281: The gradient of the log-probability for the prefix is:\nL282: \nL283:  | $$\\nabla_{\\theta}\\log p_{\\theta}(x_{0:i})=\\sum_{j=0}^{i}\\nabla_{\\theta}\\log\\pi_{\\theta}(x_{j}\\mid x_{<j}).$$  |\nL284: \nL285: Under the policy gradient update derived from (cite89†1 ), the expected change in $p_{\\theta}(x_{0:t^{\\star}})$ can be approximated by:\nL286:  | $$\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\Delta p_{\\theta}(x_{0:i})\\right]\\approx\\alpha\\cdot\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\left(\\sum_{j=0}^{i}A_{j}\\nabla_{\\theta}\\log\\pi_{\\theta}(x_{j}\\mid x_{\\leq j})\\right)^{\\top}\\nabla_{\\theta}\\log p_{\\theta}(x_{\\leq j})\\right],$$  |\nL287: \nL288: where $\\alpha$ is the learning rate. Assuming gradients for different $i$ are approximately orthogonal in expectation, we get:\nL289:  | $$\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\Delta p_{\\theta}(x_{0:i})\\right]\\approx\\alpha\\cdot\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\sum_{j=0}^{i}A_{j}\\|\\nabla_{\\theta}\\log\\pi_{\\theta}(x_{j}\\mid x_{\\leq j})\\|^{2}\\right].$$  |\nL290: \nL291: Using the empirical averages from conditions (2) and (3):\nL292:  | $\\displaystyle\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\Delta p_{\\theta}(x_{0:t^{\\star}})\\right]$  | $\\displaystyle=\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\Delta(p_{\\theta}(x_{0:t^{\\star}-1})\\cdot\\pi_{\\theta}(x_{t^{\\star}}\\mid x_{<t^{\\star}}))\\right]$  |\nL293:  |  | $\\displaystyle\\approx\\alpha\\cdot\\left(-a\\sum_{i=0}^{t^{\\star}-1}\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\|\\nabla_{\\theta}\\log\\pi_{\\theta}(x_{i}\\mid x_{<i})\\|^{2}\\right]+b\\cdot\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\|\\nabla_{\\theta}\\log\\pi_{\\theta}(x_{t^{\\star}}\\mid x_{<t^{\\star}})\\|^{2}\\right]\\right)$  |\nL294:  |  | $\\displaystyle\\approx\\alpha\\cdot\\left(-a\\cdot(t^{\\star})+b\\right)\\cdot C,$  |\nL295: where $C>0$ approximates the average squared gradient norm. When condition (4) holds ($a\\cdot t^{\\star}>b$), the term inside the parentheses is negative. Therefore:\nL296: \nL297:  | $$\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\Delta p_{\\theta}(x_{0:t^{\\star}})\\right]<0.$$  |\nL298: \nL299: Consequently, with repeated updates, the probability $p_{\\theta}(x_{0:t^{\\star}})$ tends to decrease exponentially in practice. This makes the model increasingly unlikely to generate sequences reaching position $t^{\\star}$, effectively encouraging earlier termination.\nL300: ## Appendix B Entropy Based Segmentation Strategy\nL301: \nL302: Algorithm 1 Entropy-based segmentation procedure.\nL303: \nL304: 1: Function EntropySegmentation(entropies, start_idx, outLen, max_branches=5, min_gap=10)\nL305: \nL306: 2:   if $outLen-start\\_idx<max\\_branches+1$ then\nL307: \nL308: 3:    return $(start\\_idx,outLen)$\nL309: \nL310: 4:   end if\nL311: \nL312: 5:   anchors $\\leftarrow$ top-k entropy indices in entropies[start_idx:outLen]\nL313: \nL314: 6:   anchors $\\leftarrow$ anchors + start_idx\nL315: \nL316: 7:   anchors $\\leftarrow$ sort(anchors)\nL317: \nL318: 8:   filtered $\\leftarrow[]$\nL319: 9:   for anchor in anchors (left-to-right) do\nL320: \nL321: 10:    if distance(anchor, pre-kept) $\\geq$ min_gap then\nL322: \nL323: 11:      append anchor to filtered\nL324: \nL325: 12:    end if\nL326: \nL327: 13:   end for\nL328: \nL329: 14:   cuts $\\leftarrow[]$; last_cut $\\leftarrow$ start_idx\nL330: \nL331: 15:   for $a$ in filtered do\nL332: \nL333: 16:    if $a-last\\_cut\\geq$ min_gap then\nL334: \nL335: 17:      append $a$ to cuts; last_cut $\\leftarrow a$\nL336: \nL337: 18:    end if\nL338: \nL339: 19:   end for\nL340: \nL341: 20:   segments $\\leftarrow[]$; prev $\\leftarrow$ start_idx\nL342: \nL343: 21:   for $c$ in cuts do\nL344: 22:    append $(prev,c)$ to segments; prev $\\leftarrow c$\nL345: \nL346: 23:   end for\nL347: \nL348: 24:   append $(prev,outLen)$ to segments\nL349: \nL350: 25:   sanitized $\\leftarrow[]$; cur $\\leftarrow$ start_idx\nL351: \nL352: 26:   for $(s,e)$ in segments do\nL353: \nL354: 27:    $s\\leftarrow\\max(start\\_idx,\\min(outLen,s))$\nL355: \nL356: 28:    $e\\leftarrow\\max(start\\_idx,\\min(outLen,e))$\nL357: \nL358: 29:    if $e\\leq s$ then\nL359: \nL360: 30:      continue\nL361: \nL362: 31:    end if\nL363: \nL364: 32:    if $s>cur$ then\nL365: \nL366: 33:      append $(cur,s)$ to sanitized\nL367: \nL368: 34:    end if\nL369: \nL370: 35:    append $(s,e)$ to sanitized; cur $\\leftarrow e$\nL371: 36:   end for\nL372: \nL373: 37:   if $cur<outLen$ then\nL374: \nL375: 38:    append $(cur,outLen)$ to sanitized\nL376: \nL377: 39:   end if\nL378: \nL379: 40:   if sanitized is empty then\nL380: \nL381: 41:    return $(start\\_idx,outLen)$\nL382: \nL383: 42:   else\nL384: \nL385: 43:    return sanitized\nL386: \nL387: 44:   end if\nL388: \nL389: 45: end Function\nL390: \nL391: It’s worth noting that we set k=5 and n=10 across all length of output sequences, except for one case when their is less than 5 tokens, we take the whole output sequence as one segment.\nL392: ## Appendix C Early Stop Point for Each Trainig\nL393: \nL394: ### C.1 Qwen2.5-Math-1.5B\nL395: \nL396:   * •\nL397: \nL398: GRPO: Epoch 2\nL399: \nL400:   * •\nL401: \nL402: PRM-Avg: Epoch 7\nL403: \nL404:   * •\nL405: \nL406: PURE: Epoch 10\nL407: \nL408:   * •\nL409: \nL410: GRPO+PRPO: Epoch 8\nL411: \nL412:   * •\nL413: \nL414: PRM-Avg+PRPO: Epoch 9\nL415: \nL416: ### C.2 Qwen2.5-Math-7B\nL417: \nL418:   * •\nL419: \nL420: GRPO: Epoch 9\nL421: \nL422:   * •\nL423: \nL424: PRM-Avg: Epoch 7\nL425: \nL426:   * •\nL427: \nL428: PURE: Epoch 10\nL429: \nL430:   * •\nL431: \nL432: GRPO+PRPO: Epoch 4\nL433: \nL434:   * •\nL435: \nL436: PRM-Avg+PRPO: Epoch 3\nL437: ## Appendix D Disclosure of use of AI Tools\nL438: \nL439: We used the following AI tools for this research:\nL440: \nL441:   * •\nL442: \nL443: Cursor for code revision and debugging.\nL444: \nL445:   * •\nL446: \nL447: ChatGPT for evaluation of writing and proofreading.\nL448: \nL449:   * •\nL450: \nL451: Gemini used to help design illustrations.\nL452: ## Appendix E Disclosure of license of the artifacts we used\nL453: \nL454: The artifacts we used are all open-source, and we used the following licenses:\nL455: \nL456:   * •\nL457: \nL458: The model Qwen2.5-Math-1.5B is released under the Apache License 2.0\nL459: \nL460:   * •\nL461: \nL462: The model Qwen2.5-Math-7B is released under the Apache License 2.0\nL463: \nL464:   * •\nL465: \nL466: The model Qwen2.5-Math-PRM-7B is released under the Apache License 2.0\nL467: \nL468:   * •\nL469: \nL470: The dataset MATH is released under the MIT License\nL471: \nL472:   * •\nL473: \nL474: The training framework VeRL is released under the Apache License 2.0\nL475: We strictly follow the license of the open-source artifacts we used.\nL476: \nL477: The data of AMC 2023, AIME 2025, and AIME 2024 are extracted from open website Art of Problem Solving (cite56†Art of Problem Solving, 2025a ). We strictly follow the terms of use of this website and did not use the data obtained from the website for any commercial purpose.\nL478: \nL479: Experimental support, please cite90†view the build logs for errors. Generated by cite91†L A T E xml†math.nist